Before — the rule for cancellability lives outside `Order`, reachable and re-implementable from anywhere:

```java
class Order {
    OrderStatus status;
    LocalDate shipDate;
    // getters and setters only
}

class OrderService {
    boolean canCancel(Order o) {
        return o.getStatus() != OrderStatus.SHIPPED
            && o.getShipDate().isAfter(LocalDate.now().plusDays(1));
    }
}
```

After — `Order` owns the rule about its own state:

```java
class Order {
    private OrderStatus status;
    private LocalDate shipDate;

    boolean canCancel() {
        return status != OrderStatus.SHIPPED
            && shipDate.isAfter(LocalDate.now().plusDays(1));
    }

    void cancel() {
        if (!canCancel()) throw new IllegalStateException("Order can no longer be cancelled");
        status = OrderStatus.CANCELLED;
    }
}
```

Now `RefundService` and the admin panel call `order.canCancel()` instead of reimplementing the date math — there's exactly one place the rule can drift out of sync with itself.
